2. BSP Kernel and Distros
=========================

The following table summarizes the valid Wind River Linux distros for this BSP.
'Y' in each content cell stands for supported; 'N' stands for not supported:

  +--------------+-------------+------------------+-------------------+--------------+
  | valid/distro |   wrlinux   | wrlinux-graphics | wrlinux-installer | wrlinux-tiny |
  +--------------+-------------+------------------+-------------------+--------------+
  |    valid     |      Y      |        N         |         N         |       N      |
  +--------------+-------------+------------------+-------------------+--------------+

For the supported kernel type for this BSP, check the TARGET_SUPPORTED_KTYPES
by running 'bitbake -e | grep "^TARGET_SUPPORTED_KTYPES"'.

Note: The preempt-rt ktype is available for this BSP/Machine at this time.

3. Board Specific Patches
=========================

To get a list of patches applied to the kernel specific to this BSP along with
patch descriptions use git-whatchanged on the default kernel. For example:

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/work-shared/<bsp_name>/kernel-source
	(build-host)$ git whatchanged <kernel_version>/standard/base..<kernel_version>/standard/<bsp_name>


4. Boot Instructions
====================

4.1 Device Trees
----------------
Now all Versal boards are required to use a device tree to boot Linux.
Two types of device tree files are provided and different files are
suitable for different scenarios.

4.1.1 Kernel Device Trees
-------------------------
The kernel device tree files describe the Versal board hardware features,
except for FPGA implementations. In other words, these files are only used
in scenarios without FPGA implementations.

The device tree files and their target boards are listed below:

	versal-vck190-rev1.1.dtb (used for vck190)
	versal-vmk180-rev1.1.dtb (used for vmk180)
	versal-vck190-rev1.1-x-ebm-01-revA.dtb (used for vck190 with X-EBM-01 daughter card)
	versal-vmk180-rev1.1-x-ebm-01-revA.dtb (used for vmk180 with X-EBM-01 daughter card)

They will be installed to <PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/deploy/images/amd-versal/
and the first partition of the WIC image along with the kernel Image.

The device tree source files are located at:
	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/work-shared/amd-versal/kernel-source/arch/arm64/boot/dts/xilinx

They can be modified and compiled by executing the following commands:
	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ bitbake -c devshell virtual/kernel
	(build-host)$ vim arch/arm64/boot/dts/xilinx/the_file_you_edit*.dts
	(build-host)$ make xilinx/dtb-file-name

Then the new dtb files can be found at:
	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/work/amd_versal-wrs-linux/linux-yocto/<kernel_version>/linux-amd_versal-standard-build/arch/arm64/boot/dts/xilinx/

4.1.2 system.dtb
----------------
Different from the kernel device trees above, system.dtb is used for scenarios
with FPGA. It is generated by the device-tree recipe in meta-xilinx. Its
principle is to generate system.dtb based on board dts files downloaded from
https://github.com/Xilinx/device-tree-xlnx.git and Programming Logic dtsi file
pl.dtsi which is extracted from the Xilinx Support Archive(xsa) file.

If "DISTRO_FEATURES:append = ' xilinx-bootloader'" is added to conf/local.conf,
the device-tree recipe will generate system.dtb, install it to
<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/deploy/images/amd-versal/ and the first partition of
the WIC image.

The system.dtb should be used as long as the system involves FPGA implementation.

4.2 Boot Mode
-------------
The Versal boards support three boot modes: SD card, QSPI32 and JTAG.

4.2.1 SD Boot Mode
------------------
First refer to the section "7.1 BOOT.BIN" to build a bootable WIC
image and burn it to SD card at host-pc:

   (build-host)$ sudo dd if=wrlinux-image-<rootfs_type>-amd-versal.rootfs.wic of=/dev/sdaX

Plug the SD card into the Versal board SD socket and set the DIP
switch SW1 setting like:

   SW1[4]  SW1[3]  SW1[2]  SW1[1]
   OFF     OFF     OFF     ON

Power on the Versal boards or press the POR button SW2.

4.2.2 QSPI32 Boot Mode
----------------------
First we boot the board with SD Card by following the steps in the
section "4.2.1 SD Boot Mode". At the U-Boot stage, when the message
"Hit any key to stop autoboot:" appears, hit any key, then run the
following commands to flash the images on the QSPI daughter card:

   (u-boot)> sf probe 0 0 0
   (u-boot)> sf erase 0x0 0x4000000
   (u-boot)> fatls mmc 0:1
   (u-boot)> fatload mmc 0:1 0x70000000 BOOT.bin
   (u-boot)> sf write 0x70000000 0x0 <BOOT.BIN_filesize_in_hex>

Note that the "<BOOT.BIN_filesize_in_hex>" is a hexadecimal value of
the size of BOOT.BIN.

After flashing the images, turn off the power switch on the board,
and change the SW1 boot mode pin settings to QSPI boot mode:

   SW1[4]  SW1[3]  SW1[2]  SW1[1]
   ON      ON      OFF     ON

Power cycle the board. The board now boots up using the BOOT.BIN in
the QSPI flash.

4.2.3 JTAG Boot Mode
--------------------
The JTAG Boot Mode mainly uses xsct on host-pc and it is out of the
scope of this README. Please refer to the following link or other
documents:

	https://xilinx-wiki.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/A/pages/2037088327/Versal+Platform+Loader+and+Manager

4.3 Boot Linux From U-Boot
--------------------------
This section provides the examples about how to setup u-boot to boot
Linux via network and SD Card.

4.3.1 Network
-------------
First you will need a host machine with the network servers TFTP and NFS
correctly installed and configured.

First find the compressed archive rootfs file at:
	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/deploy/images/amd-versal/wrlinux-image-<rootfs_type>-amd-versal.rootfs.tar.bz2

Then extract this file to <nfsroot_path>:
   (build-host)$ sudo tar jxf wrlinux-image-<rootfs_type>-amd-versal.rootfs.tar.bz2 -C <nfsroot_path>

And copy the kernel and dtb file to <tftpserver_path>:
   (build-host)$ cp Image <tftpserver_path>
   (build-host)$ cp system.dtb <tftpserver_path>

Set the u-boot environment variables and boot:
   (u-boot)> setenv serverip <tftpserver_ip>; setenv ipaddr <board_ip>
   (u-boot)> setenv bootargs console=ttyAMA0 earlycon=pl011,mmio32,0xFF000000,115200n8 clk_ignore_unused root=/dev/nfs rw nfsroot=$serverip:<nfsroot_path> ip=dhcp
   (u-boot)> tftpboot 0x10000000 Image; tftpboot 0x13800000 system.dtb; booti 0x10000000 - 0x13800000

4.3.2 SD Card
-------------
If DISTRO_FEATURES contains "xilinx-bootloader", there is a boot.scr that
contains U-Boot commands to boot Linux from SD Card automatically. There
is no need to reconfigure u-boot.

If DISTRO_FEATURES does not contain "xilinx-bootloader", execute the following
commands to set the u-boot environment variables and boot:
   (u-boot)> setenv bootfile Image; setenv fdtfile dtb-file-name; setenv loadaddr 0x10000000; setenv fdtaddr 0x18800000;
   (u-boot)> setenv bootargs 'root=/dev/mmcblk0p2 rw console=ttyAMA0 earlycon=pl011,mmio32,0xFF000000,115200n8 clk_ignore_unused rootwait'
   (u-boot)> setenv bootcmd 'fatload mmc 0:1 $loadaddr $bootfile; fatload mmc 0:1 $fdtaddr $fdtfile; booti $loadaddr - $fdtaddr';
   (u-boot)> saveenv; run bootcmd;


5. WIC Notes
============

Users can use the OpenEmbedded Image Creator to create the properly partitioned
image on a SD card. It generates partitioned images from existing OpenEmbedded
build artifacts. Please refer to the following URL for more detailed partition
information about WIC:

https://docs.yoctoproject.org/singleindex.html#creating-partitioned-images-using-wic

This BSP supports disk images for SD card.
After building the project, users will get a WIC image under the directory
tmp/deploy/images/<bsp name>/, such as:

	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/deploy/images/amd-versal/wrlinux-image-<rootfs_type>-amd-versal.rootfs.wic

NOTE: The WIC image built in WRLinux does not contain the bootloader BOOT.BIN
by default. Please refer to the section 7.1 "BOOT.BIN" for the detailed
description about how to create a bootable WIC image.

To burn WIC image to SD card, users only needs to execute the below command:

	(build-host)$ sudo dd if=wrlinux-image-<rootfs_type>-amd-versal.rootfs.wic of=/dev/your_sd_dev


6. Features
===========

6.1 kexec and kdump
-------------------

You need to add feature/kexec support as follows:
	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
WRTEMPLATE = "feature/kexec,feature/kdump"
_EOF

6.1.1 kexec
-----------

	root@amd-versal:~# kexec -l  /boot/Image --append="`cat /proc/cmdline`"
	root@amd-versal:~# kexec -e

6.1.2 kdump
-----------

Add crashkernel=512M to the kernel cmdline
	root@amd-versal:~# kexec -p /boot/Image --append="$your-bootcmd"
	root@amd-versal:~# echo c > /proc/sysrq-trigger

6.2 System Monitor
------------------

System monitor includes PL monitor and PS monitor.
Each system monitor measures voltage and temperature to provide information
and alarms to other parts of the system including the PMU, RPU, and APU
processors.

Customers can check the detailed system monitor interfaces with the following
command:
	root@amd-versal:~# ls -la /sys/bus/iio/devices/iio:device0

6.3 QSPI-NOR Flash
------------------
There is QSPI-NOR flash at the X-EBM-01 QSPI external daughter card, please make
sure that the X-EBM-01 card is installed on J212 of the VCK190/VMK180 boards.

If DISTRO_FEATURES contains "xilinx-bootloader", system.dtb has already contained
the flash device node by following the commands in the sections "7.1 BOOT.BIN" and
"7.2 Workflows".

If DISTRO_FEATURES does not contain "xilinx-bootloader", the following dtb files
should be used:
	versal-vck190-rev1.1-x-ebm-01-revA.dtb (for vck190 board)
	versal-vmk180-rev1.1-x-ebm-01-revA.dtb (for vmk180 board)

They can be found at the path:
	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp-glibc/deploy/images/amd-versal/

After Linux boots successfully, list recognized MTD devices to ensure the QSPI-NOR
flash is present:
	root@amd-versal:~# cat /proc/mtd
	dev:    size   erasesize  name
	mtd0: 10000000 00020000 "spi0-flash0"

6.4 CANFD
---------

There is one canfd device interface J5 on the board. Connect the canfd interfaces
of 2 boards and use the following verification commands:

VCK190/VMK180 board-1:
	root@amd-versal:~# ip link set can0 up type can bitrate 125000 dbitrate 8000000 fd on
	root@amd-versal:~# candump can0 &
	root@amd-versal:~# ip -d -s link show can0

VCK190/VMK180 board-2:
	root@amd-versal:~# ip link set can0 up type can bitrate 125000 dbitrate 8000000 fd on
	root@amd-versal:~# cansend can0 5A1#11.22.33.44.55.66.77.88
	root@amd-versal:~# cansend can0 5A1##000112233445566778899aabbccddeeff
	root@amd-versal:~# ip -d -s link show can0

If can0 at board-1 can dump data from board-2, CANFD feature works fine.

6.5 USB
-------

For VMK180/VCK190 board, the USB controller can be configured as host, device modes.

6.5.1 USB Host
--------------

For Host mode, short the jumper 1-2 of J308:
	J308
	1-2 3

6.5.2 USB Device
----------------

For Device mode, open the jumper 1-2 of J308:
	J308
	1 2 3

In addition to the method which modifies "dr_mode" property in dts file, you can change this property
by using "fdt" u-boot command. The example for USB device mode is as described below:

    (u-boot)> tftpboot 0x10000000 Image; tftpboot 0x18800000 dtb
    (u-boot)> fdt addr 0x18800000; fdt resize; fdt set /axi/usb@ff9d0000/usb@fe200000 dr_mode peripheral
    (u-boot)> booti 0x10000000 - 0x18800000

Use the below example settings for configuring USB as MASS STORAGE profile to verify the device mode:

	root@amd-versal:~# dd if=/dev/zero of=/tmp/mydev count=500 bs=1M
	root@amd-versal:~# cd /sys/kernel/config/usb_gadget
	root@amd-versal:~# mkdir g1
	root@amd-versal:~# cd g1
	root@amd-versal:~# echo "64" > bMaxPacketSize0
	root@amd-versal:~# echo "0x200" > bcdUSB
	root@amd-versal:~# echo "0x100" > bcdDevice
	root@amd-versal:~# echo "0x03FD" > idVendor
	root@amd-versal:~# echo "0x0501" > idProduct
	root@amd-versal:~# cat max_speed
	root@amd-versal:~# echo "high-speed" > max_speed
	root@amd-versal:~# mkdir functions/mass_storage.ms0
	root@amd-versal:~# mkdir configs/c1.1
	root@amd-versal:~# echo /tmp/mydev > functions/mass_storage.ms0/lun.0/file
	root@amd-versal:~# echo 1 > functions/mass_storage.ms0/lun.0/removable
	root@amd-versal:~# ln -s functions/mass_storage.ms0/ configs/c1.1/
	root@amd-versal:~# echo "fe200000.usb" > UDC

Connect the cable from board to Windows or Linux host machine, and the mass storage device
can be observed.

6.6 Suspend/Resume
------------------
6.6.1 Suspend
-------------
The Versal platforms support the following sleep states:
    root@amd-versal:~# cat /sys/power/state
    freeze mem

Enter the "mem" sleep state:
	root@amd-versal:~# echo mem > /sys/power/state

6.6.2 Resume
------------
To wake up the system from sleep state, customers can use RTC or serial
as a wakeup source.

Run the following commands to enable the serial port as a wakeup source:
    root@amd-versal:~# echo enabled > /sys/class/tty/ttyAMA0/power/wakeup

Run the following commands to wake up the system after an 8-second sleep:
    root@amd-versal:~# rtcwake -v -s 8 -m mem

6.7 LPD I2C
-----------
In SDK's board dts files, I2C devices are not described. This is because I2C
buses on the Versal boards are designed so that multiple masters have access
to them. One way is from Versal and the second from System Controller.
And because on I2C there are I2C muxes which are not safe for multiple masters
access so the I2C buses are described only from System Controller's point of
view. And it is up to the users to decide if it is safe to enable the specific
I2C devices and also it depends on tasks running on SC.

For WRLinux, we have enabled the I2C device nodes in the kernel device trees
and system.dtb by default. If customers want to disable these I2C devices,
modifications on relevant board dts file will be required.

For kernel device trees, the dts files enabling I2C devices are located at:
	<PROJECT_DIR>/build/tmp/work-shared/amd-versal/kernel-source/arch/arm64/boot/dts/xilinx

For system.dtb, the dts files enabling I2C devices are located at:
	<PROJECT_DIR>/layers/amd-versal/recipes-bsp/device-tree/files

7 Xilinx Specific Features
==========================

7.1 BOOT.BIN
------------
BOOT.BIN is the unified boot image format for AMD Versal ACAP
(Adaptive Compute Acceleration Platform) devices. Versal introduces
a revolutionary heterogeneous architecture combining scalar engines
(ARM Cortex-A72/R5F), adaptable engines (programmable logic), and
intelligent engines (AI Engine array), all managed through an advanced
boot sequence.

BOOT.BIN Detailed Structure:
	BOOT.BIN
	├── Boot Header
	├── PLM (Platform Loader Manager)
	├── PSM Firmware (Platform Security Manager)
	├── Programming Device Image (PDI)
	├── Configuration Data Objects (CDO)
	├── AI Engine Partition (Optional)
	├── ATF (ARM Trusted Firmware)
	└── Application Processors
	    └── U-Boot (Secondary bootloader)

There are two ways to generate BOOT.BIN:
	- Using prebuilt firmwares
	- Using WRLinux

7.1.1 Prebuilt Firmwares
------------------------
If you just need a bootable BOOT.BIN to boot Linux, but do not want to compile
it, you can use the prebuilt firmwares provided by AMD to generate one directly.
The following example shows the steps for generating BOOT.BIN for vmk180. The
steps for vck190 are very similar.

	(build-host)$ git clone https://github.com/Xilinx/soc-prebuilt-firmware.git
	(build-host)$ cd soc-prebuilt-firmware
	(build-host)$ git checkout xlnx_rel_v2025.1
	(build-host)$ cd vmk180-versal
	(build-host)$ bootgen -arch versal -image bootgen.bif -o ./BOOT.BIN -w

The bootgen tool used above is in <path_to_Vivado>/bin/bootgen. If you do not
have Vivado/Vitis installed, you can download the source code of bootgen, compile
it, and then use it after installation.

	(build-host)$ git clone https://github.com/Xilinx/bootgen.git
	(build-host)$ cd bootgen; git checkout xlnx_rel_v2025.1
	(build-host)$ make; make install

The generated BOOT.BIN can be directly copied to the first FAT32 partition
of the SD card or to be installed to the WIC image by executing the following
steps:

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
IMAGE_BOOT_FILES:append = " /< path_to_BOOT.BIN >/BOOT.BIN;BOOT.BIN"
_EOF
	(build-host)$ bitbake wrlinux-image-std

7.1.2 WRLinux
-------------
WRLinux uses the recipes from meta-xilinx to automate the entire process
by building all required components, generating the appropriate BIF file
and calling bootgen to create the final BOOT.BIN image.

The following steps are to generate BOOT.BIN by using a default XSA file
downloaded from AMD Website by the external-hdf recipe. And if customers
own a customized XSA file which contains a specific FPGA implementation
to generate a BOOT.BIN, please refer to the section "7.2 Workflows".

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'bootgen'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx-scr'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'xilinx-bootbin'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'psmfw'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'plmfw'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'base-pdi'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'extract-cdo'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'trusted-firmware-a'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'device-tree'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-tools += 'external-hdf'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'psm-firmware'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'plm-firmware'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'embeddedsw-source'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-tools-xlnx'
PREFERRED_PROVIDER_u-boot-tools-native = "u-boot-tools-xlnx-native"
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-tools += 'xsct'
BB_NO_NETWORK = '0'
# change to "vck190" for vck190 board
VERSAL_TARGET_BOARD = "vmk180"
DISTRO_FEATURES:append = " xilinx-bootloader"
_EOF
	(build-host)$ bitbake wrlinux-image-std

7.2 Workflows
-------------
When customers complete their system designs via Vivado/Vitis, an XSA
file is generated. The XSA (Xilinx Shell Archive) file is a container
that describes a hardware platform. This file can be used to generate
necessary hardware and software components that are then integrated
into a Yocto-based Linux build through XSCT/SDT workflows.

According to how the Programmable Logic (PL) portion of the XSA is
structured and reconfigured, the XSA files can be divided into three
types:
	-Flat Configuration
	-Segment Configuration
	-Dynamic Function eXchange(DFX)
Different XSA types will lead to different steps for each workflow.

The DFX XSA is NOT supported by both workflows yet.

This section will use the VCK190 board as an example. For the VMK180 board,
just replace the string "vck190" in the command lines with "vmk180".

Note that AMD is phasing out the XSCT workflow in favor of the SDT workflow
for new projects. Therefore, we strongly recommend that customers switch
to the SDT workflow as soon as possible.

7.2.1 gen-machineconf
---------------------
Both XSCT and SDT workflows require the use of the tool gen-machineconf.
For a more detailed description of gen-machineconf, please see
	https://github.com/Xilinx/gen-machine-conf/blob/rel-v2025.1/README.md

7.2.2 XSCT Workflow
-------------------
The XSCT workflow requires an XSA file as input. For a more detailed description
of the XSCT workflow with Yocto, please see
	https://github.com/Xilinx/meta-xilinx-tools/blob/rel-v2025.1/README.xsct.bsp.md

Note that there is a requirement to install libtinfo.so.5 for the xsct tool.
Please ensure that the package libtinfo5 has been installed to host os.

Executing the following commands to start the XSCT workflow:

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:openembedded-layer += 'kconfig-frontends'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:meta-python += 'python3-humanfriendly'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:virtualization-layer += 'lopper'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'python3-anytree'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'xilinx-lops'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'esw-conf'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-tools += 'xsct'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'embeddedsw-source'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'bootgen'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx-scr'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'xilinx-bootbin'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'psmfw'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'plmfw'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'base-pdi'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'extract-cdo'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'trusted-firmware-a'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'device-tree'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-tools += 'external-hdf'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'psm-firmware'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'plm-firmware'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-tools-xlnx'
PREFERRED_PROVIDER_u-boot-tools-native = "u-boot-tools-xlnx-native"
VERSAL_TARGET_BOARD = "vck190"
DISTRO_FEATURES:append = " xilinx-bootloader"
XILINX_WITH_ESW = "xsct"
BB_NO_NETWORK = '0'
_EOF
	(build-host)$ export PATH=$PATH:<PROJECT_DIR>/layers/meta-xilinx/gen-machine-conf/

For the Flat Configuration XSA, you need execute:
	(build-host)$ gen-machineconf parse-xsa --hw-description <path_to_xsa-file/proj-flat.xsa> -c conf/ --machine-name versal-vck190-xsct --add-config CONFIG_SUBSYSTEM_MACHINE_NAME=\"versal-vck190-reva-x-ebm-01-reva\"
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
WORKFLOW_MACHINE_NAME = "versal-vck190-xsct"
_EOF
	(build-host)$ bitbake wrlinux-image-std

For the Segment Configuration XSA:
	(build-host)$ gen-machineconf parse-xsa --hw-description <path_to_xsa-file/proj-seg.xsa> -c conf/ --machine-name versal-vck190-xsct-seg --add-config CONFIG_SUBSYSTEM_MACHINE_NAME=\"versal-vck190-reva-x-ebm-01-reva\" --add-config CONFIG_SUBSYSTEM_FPGA_MANAGER=y
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
WORKFLOW_MACHINE_NAME = "versal-vck190-xsct-seg"
_EOF
	(build-host)$ bitbake wrlinux-image-std

NOTE:
If you need to re-run gen-machineconf under an old project, you must clean
the old project first:
	(build-host)$ bitbake -c clean device-tree base-pdi psm-firmware plm-firmware
	(build-host)$ rm conf/machine -Rf; rm output -Rf; rm conf/dts -Rf; rm hw-description -Rf
	(build-host)$ sed -i '/WORKFLOW_MACHINE_NAME/d' conf/local.conf
And after re-running the gen-machineconf command, do not forget to add
WORKFLOW_MACHINE_NAME back to conf/local.conf according to the type of
XSA file.

7.2.3 SDT Workflow
------------------
The SDT workflow uses the output of "SDTGen" as input. For a more detailed
description of the SDT workflow with Yocto, please see
	https://github.com/Xilinx/meta-xilinx/blob/rel-v2025.1/meta-xilinx-standalone-sdt/README.sdt.bsp.md

The System Device Tree Generator (SDTGen) is a tool designed by AMD to convert
its proprietary output formats from the AMD Vivado Design Suite into a
comprehensive system device tree.
For a more detailed description of sdtgen, please see
	https://xilinx-wiki.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/A/pages/3156475927/Overview+of+System+Device+Tree+Generator+SDTGen

The following commands should be executed at a "build-host" with Vivado installed:
	(build-host)$ mkdir <my-project-dir>
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> sdt.tcl
set outdir [lindex $argv 1]
set xsa [lindex $argv 0]
exec rm -rf $outdir
sdtgen set_dt_param -zocl enable -debug enable -xsa $xsa -dir $outdir -board_dts versal-vck190-reva-x-ebm-01-reva
sdtgen generate_sdt
_EOF
	(build-host)$ xsct sdt.tcl <path_to_xsa-file/project.xsa> <my-project-dir>

Make sure that the folder <my-project-dir> is placed where WRLinux can access it
and start SDT workflow by executing the following commands:

	(build-host)$ cd <PROJECT_DIR>; . ./environment-setup-x86_64-wrlinuxsdk-linux; . ./oe-init-build-env
	(build-host)$ cat << _EOF >> conf/local.conf
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:openembedded-layer += 'kconfig-frontends'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:meta-python += 'python3-humanfriendly'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:virtualization-layer += 'lopper'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'python3-anytree'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'xilinx-lops'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'esw-conf'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'embeddedsw-source'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'bootgen'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx-scr'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-xlnx'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'xilinx-bootbin'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'psmfw'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'plmfw'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'base-pdi'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'extract-cdo'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'trusted-firmware-a'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'device-tree'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'psm-firmware'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone += 'plm-firmware'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx += 'u-boot-tools-xlnx'
CUSTOMER_RECIPES:xilinx-standalone-sdt += 'sdt-artifacts aiefal aienginev2 audio-formatter avbuf axicdma axicdma-example axidma axidma-example axiethernet axiethernet-example axipcie axipmon axipmon-example axis-switch axivdma axivdma-example bram bram-example can can-example canfd canfd-example canps canps-example cframe cfupmc clk-wiz clk-wiz-example clockps clockps-example common coresightps-dcc csi csi2tx csi2txss csudma csudma-example dcsub ddrcpsu devcfg devcfg-example dfeccf dfeccf-example dfeequ dfeequ-example dfemix dfemix-example dfeofdm dfeofdm-example dfeprach dfeprach-example dfxasm dfxasm-example dmaps dmaps-example dp12 dp12rxss dp12txss dp14 dp14rxss dp14txss dp21 dp21rxss dp21txss dpdma dphy dppsu dsi dsi2rx dsirxss dsitxss dual-splitter emaclite emaclite-example emacps emacps-example emc gpio gpio-example gpiops gpiops-example hdcp1x hdcp22-cipher hdcp22-cipher-dp hdcp22-common hdcp22-mmult hdcp22-rng hdcp22-rx hdcp22-rx-dp hdcp22-tx hdcp22-tx-dp hwicap i2srx i2stx i3c i3cpsx i3cpsx-example iic iic-example iicps iicps-example intc intc-example iomodule ipipsu ipipsu-example llfifo llfifo-example mbox mboxpsu-exampmcdma mcdma-example mipicsiss mipi-rx-phy mipi-tx-phy mmidp mutex mutex-example nandps nandpsu nandpsu-example ospipsv ospipsv-example pciepsu pciepsu-example prc prd qspips qspips-example qspipsu qspipsu-example resetps resetps-example rfdc rfdc-example rtcpsu rtcpsu-example scugic scugic-example scutimer scuwdt sd-fec sdiaud sdi-common sdps sdps-example spdif spi spips spips-example sysmon sysmon-example sysmonpsu sysmonpsu-example sysmonpsv sysmonpsv-example tft tmrctr tmrctr-example tmr-inject tmr-manager trafgen trafgen-example trngpsv trngpsx ttcps ttcps-example uartlite uartlite-example uartns550 uartns550-example uartps uartps-example uartpsv uartpsv-example ufspsxc usb usb-example usbps usbpsu usbpsu-example v-axi4s-remap v-csc v-deinterlacer v-demosaic v-demosaict v-gamma-lfrmbuf-rd v-frmbuf-rd-example v-frmbuf-wr v-frmbuf-wr-exa